Z||000000||enslaved||09-03-2001||10:43 PM||||Well, i've heard this issue discussed many times before. My pedal almost goes to the floor before the brakes start to grab. I just bled all 4 calipers too. Is there a EASY way to fix this? I dont exactly understand the concept behind that shim thing that theogre was talking about. BTW, my e-brake cables are froze.. so i left the springs on the calipers, but took the cables off. But the pedal was like this before i changed the pads, turned the rotors, and bled the calipers. So, how do i fix it?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||8
Z||000001||fiero_hunter85||09-03-2001||10:46 PM||||its probably a bad master cylinder or a worn rubber brake line where it allows expansion of one of the rubber brake lines.||||reg||1
Z||000002||enslaved||09-03-2001||11:00 PM||||Well, i wouldn't think the m/c would be bad. The brakes still work, the pedal just travels far. Like the pads are far away from the rotor. My 84 SE only has 45k miles on it. I doubt the brake lines are shot. Im going to play with the e-brake i guess. Maybe put the cables back on, and pull out the e-brake lever just enough to push that piston out to decrease pedal travel. Who knows. maybe it will work. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||09-03-2001||11:09 PM||||Doing what you are describing will likely hang the caliper or drag the rear brakes and cook them off in short order.<P>It could be the MC or the rear calipers. If you don't understand the adjustment procedure then find someone that does. It's the only way you will get the system to work right without a working hand brake. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||09-04-2001||07:50 AM||||When you rotate the e-brake lever on the caliper do the pads make contact with the rotor?  If not then you need to adjust the e-brake mechanism.  With or without the cables installed the e-brake must be adjusted so the pads start to engage when the lever is rotated but fully disengage when the lever is allowed to return to its stop.  This takes the slack out of the piston travel and will result in less pedal travel before pad engagement.<P>If rotating the lever does not result in pads engaging the rotor remove the e-brake cable and spring and the lever from the shaft.  Now you can use the lever to screw the shaft into the piston bore.  Rotate the shaft to its full travel.  If the pads still don't engage take the lever off again and rotate it some more.  If I remember correctly the lever has a hexagonal hole where it contacts the shoulder of the shaft.<P>Keep doing this to screw the piston into the bore until you finally get the pads to contact the rotor.  You may have to back off a bit if you can't see the pads fully dinengaging when you let the lever rotate back against the stop as when the e-brake is released.<p>[This message has been edited by Carrolles (edited 09-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||09-04-2001||08:43 PM||||The adjustment procedure now has some editing and better/more pictures.<P>The shim is about the only way to be sure exactly what the clearance is set to. Too much = low pedal. To little and you could drag the brakes.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ka4nkf||09-04-2001||10:38 PM||||If you go by OGRES instructions I will bet that will work. He is absolutely correct on his procedure. I know because my brakes are really good now with good pedal. If this does not work then my next step would be a master cylinder.||||reg||1
